menu "SPI NAND Flash configuration"

    config NAND_FLASH_VERIFY_WRITE
        bool "Verify SPI NAND flash writes"
        default n
        help
            If this option is enabled, any time SPI NAND flash is written then the data will be read
            back and verified. This can catch hardware problems with SPI NAND flash, or flash which
            was not erased before verification.

    config NAND_FLASH_ENABLE_BDL
        bool "Enable Block Device Layer (BDL) support"
        depends on IDF_INIT_VERSION >= "6.0"
        default n
        help
            Enable Block Device Layer (BDL) support for SPI NAND Flash. This provides:
            - Standard esp_blockdev_t interface for layered architecture
            - Flash Block Device Layer (raw NAND flash access)
            - Wear-Leveling Block Device Layer (logical sector access with wear leveling)
            - Advanced layered API (spi_nand_flash_init_with_layers)
            When disabled, only the legacy API is available.
            When enabled, the legacy spi_nand_flash_init_device() is not available (returns
            ESP_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED). Use spi_nand_flash_init_with_layers().

            Note: This option requires ESP-IDF >= 6.0 (esp_blockdev component).
            Enabling this on ESP-IDF < 6.0 will result in a build error.

    config NAND_FLASH_PAGE_REGISTER_CACHE
        bool "Enable NAND page-register cache"
        default n
        help
            When enabled, the SPI NAND driver tracks whether the chip's
            internal page register already holds the requested page and
            skips the READ PAGE ADDRESS command (25-100 us) on repeated
            reads of the same page. The cache is invalidated on any
            program, erase, or mark-bad operation, since those overwrite
            the chip's internal register.
            Set to false to disable (no caching, original behaviour).

    config NAND_DHARA_FTL_MAP_PATH_CACHE
        bool "Enable Dhara FTL map path cache"
        default n
        help
            When enabled, Dhara FTL map caches the last successful read-only
            FTL map traversal. Consumes 136 bytes of memory. Consecutive
            sector lookups that share a common radix-tree prefix skip the
            already-known levels entirely, reducing Dhara FTL journal read meta
            calls by up to 31 of 32 for sequential sector access patterns.
            Set to false to disable (no RAM overhead, original behaviour).

    config NAND_DHARA_FTL_MAP_EXACT_REPEAT_CACHE
        bool "Enable Dhara FTL map exact-repeat fast path"
        depends on NAND_DHARA_FTL_MAP_PATH_CACHE
        default n
        help
            When enabled (on top of NAND_DHARA_FTL_MAP_PATH_CACHE), an exact
            repeat of the previous read-only sector lookup (same sector,
            journal unchanged since) skips the metadata read and radix-tree
            walk entirely and returns the cached result page directly.
            Consumes 4 extra bytes of memory on top of the path cache.
            Set to false to disable (path cache still walks one metadata
            read for a repeated lookup).

    config NAND_DHARA_JOURNAL_META_CACHE_SLOTS
        int "Dhara journal metadata checkpoint-page cache slots (0 = disabled)"
        range 0 8
        default 0
        help
            Number of full NAND pages cached in internal DRAM by the Dhara
            journal layer (below the FTL map caches above) to accelerate
            repeated dhara_journal_read_meta() reads from the same
            checkpoint group. On a cache miss, the whole checkpoint page is
            loaded and kept (not just the requested metadata slice), since
            on SPI NAND the READ PAGE ADDRESS command dominates read cost
            regardless of how many bytes are transferred -- so the full-page
            load amortises across every metadata slot within that
            checkpoint group. Cache entries are invalidated by key
            (physical checkpoint-page address) whenever that same page is
            rewritten or the journal is cleared, so a stale physical
            address can never be served after reuse.
            Each slot costs one NAND page (typically 2048 bytes) of
            DMA-aligned internal DRAM. Total cost: SLOTS x page_size bytes.
            Set to 0 to disable (no RAM overhead, original behaviour).

    config NAND_ENABLE_STATS
        bool "Host test statistics enabled"
        depends on IDF_TARGET_LINUX
        default n
        help
            This option enables gathering host test statistics and SPI NAND flash wear levelling simulation.

endmenu
